   "outputs": [],
   "source": [
    "# Price with calibrated model\ncalibrated_pricer = HestonPricer(S0=100, r=0.05, kappa=kappa_cal, theta=theta_cal,\n",
    "                               sigma_v=sigma_v_cal, rho=rho_cal, v0=v0_cal)\ncalibrated_prices = np.array([calibrated_pricer.call_price_fft(K, 0.25) for K in market_strikes])\n",
    "\n# Plot comparison\nfig, axes = plt.subplots(1, 2, figsize=(14, 5))\n",
    "\n# Plot 1: Price comparison\nax = axes[0]\nax.scatter(market_strikes, market_prices, s=100, alpha=0.7, color='red', label='Market (observed)', zorder=3)\nax.plot(market_strikes, calibrated_prices, 'o-', linewidth=2, markersize=6, color='blue', label='Calibrated Model')\nax.set_xlabel('Strike Price', fontsize=11)\nax.set_ylabel('Option Price ($)', fontsize=11)\nax.set_title('Market Prices vs Calibrated Heston Model', fontsize=12, fontweight='bold')\nax.legend(fontsize=10)\nax.grid(True, alpha=0.3)\n",
    "\n# Plot 2: Pricing errors\nax = axes[1]\nerrors = (calibrated_prices - market_prices)\nax.bar(range(len(market_strikes)), errors, color=['red' if e < 0 else 'blue' for e in errors], alpha=0.7)\nax.set_xlabel('Option Index', fontsize=11)\nax.set_ylabel('Model Price - Market Price ($)', fontsize=11)\nax.set_title('Calibration Errors (by option)', fontsize=12, fontweight='bold')\nax.axhline(y=0, color='black', linestyle='-', linewidth=1)\nax.grid(True, alpha=0.3, axis='y')\n",
    "\nplt.tight_layout()\nplt.savefig('heston_calibration.png', dpi=150, bbox_inches='tight')\nplt.show()\n\nprint(f\"\\nCalibration RMSE: ${rmse:.4f}\")\nprint(f\"Mean Absolute Error: ${np.mean(np.abs(errors)):.4f}\")"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "## Conclusions\n",
    "\n",
    "1. **Stochastic volatility is essential**: The Heston model explains the \"volatility smile\"—observed in real markets but impossible in Black-Scholes.\n",
    "\n",
    "2. **Characteristic functions enable efficient computation**: FFT-based pricing is orders of magnitude faster than Monte Carlo simulation.\n",
    "\n",
    "3. **Calibration is the hard part**: Fitting realistic parameters requires sophisticated optimization (global methods like differential evolution).\n",
    "\n",
    "4. **No perfect fit**: Even after calibration, models leave residual bid-ask spreads—this is where traders make money.\n",
    "\n",
    "### Key Insight\n",
    "> **\"Option pricing theory provides elegant mathematical frameworks, but **practical trading is about identifying mispricings**: where model-implied prices differ from market prices due to estimation error, market frictions, or model misspecification.\"**\n"   ]
  }
